# For a description of the syntax of this configuration file,
# see the file kconfig-language.txt in the NuttX tools repository.
#

config LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_AUDIO_TEST
	bool "BK7258 internal-DAC audio bring-up test"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_AUDIO
	---help---
		Enable the audio_test NSH command: plays a synthesised tone through
		the internal DAC, captures the analog MIC channels, and reports
		per-channel DC/RMS/peak plus the correlation between the microphone
		and the MIC2 post-amplifier echo reference.

		This is a measurement command rather than a player or recorder: the
		AP configuration has no writable filesystem, so there is nowhere to
		store a WAV file.

config AUDIO_TEST_OGG_COMPLEXITY
	int "Opus encoder complexity (0-10)"
	default 0
	range 0 10
	---help---
		Complexity passed to OPUS_SET_COMPLEXITY by the shared Ogg Opus
		encoder in audio_test_ogg.c, which both the audio_test command and
		the VelaSight social session use.

		Deliberately not libopus's own default of 9.  That default assumes
		a host encoding far faster than realtime; this board does not.
		Measured 2026-09-07 during a continuous social session, encoding
		each 2 s chunk took about 3.2 s of dedicated CPU -- the encoder was
		60% short of keeping up with the microphone, and 45% of the
		conversation was lost to the capture ring overflowing while it
		worked.

		0 is the cheapest setting and typically several times faster than
		9.  What it gives up is rate-distortion efficiency, which matters
		least in this application: 16 kHz mono speech at 24 kbps on its way
		to a recogniser, where the alternative is missing half of it.

		Raise it only after measuring that the encoder keeps ahead of the
		microphone -- the "microphone: ... N bytes dropped" line at the end
		of a session is the test.
